WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.412
This example intentionally fails. The active-patient view shows only active rows and includes a check option.

00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:12.292
The update tries to set an active patient to inactive through that view.

00:00:12.292 --> 00:00:18.722
Because the resulting row would no longer satisfy the view condition, PostgreSQL rejects the update.

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:20.967
This is expected behavior.

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:27.837
Some simple views can be updated because PostgreSQL can map each view row back to one base-table row.

00:00:27.837 --> 00:00:34.547
Filtered views introduce a policy question: should an update be allowed to move a row outside the view?

00:00:34.547 --> 00:00:38.560
WITH CHECK OPTION prevents writes that violate the view's own filter.
